From: PS3 Custom Toolchain
Subject: [PATCH] rs6000: tag cell64lv2 objects with their data model

ILP32 and LP64 objects for CellOS Lv-2 are both ELFCLASS64 with the same
e_flags, so nothing in an object said which data model it was built
for, and an -mlp64 link that picked up an ILP32 library linked it
silently and crashed at run time.

Emit ".gnu_attribute 16, 1" (ILP32) or ".gnu_attribute 16, 2" (LP64) at
the end of every cell64lv2 assembly file.  binutils patch 0006 names the
tag Tag_GNU_Power_CellOS_Data_Model and makes ld refuse a link whose
tagged inputs disagree.  .gnu.attributes is a non-allocated section that
programs passing floating-point values already carried (the Power FP
tag); an integer-only program now gains it too.  It is never loaded.

--- a/gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000.cc
+++ b/gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000.cc
@@ -21039,6 +21039,13 @@
 		 aix_struct_return ? 2 : 1);
     }
 #endif
+#ifdef POWERPC_CELL64LV2
+  /* Tag_GNU_Power_CellOS_Data_Model: 1 for ILP32, 2 for LP64.  The two
+     data models share one ELF class and e_flags, so this tag is how ld
+     tells an ILP32 object in an LP64 link (or the reverse) and refuses
+     the link instead of producing an executable that crashes.  */
+  fprintf (asm_out_file, "\t.gnu_attribute 16, %d\n", TARGET_LP64 ? 2 : 1);
+#endif
 #if defined (POWERPC_LINUX) || defined (POWERPC_FREEBSD)
   if (TARGET_32BIT || DEFAULT_ABI == ABI_ELFv2)
     file_end_indicate_exec_stack ();
